From 44793a0b45b19192388e33ff1003f704d997270f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tendai Makumire <121951760+tmakumire@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 31 Jul 2026 17:08:11 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Add Type-C Mux as a HPD device in the DP stack (#1)

This commit adds support for using a Type-C Mux as a
Hot-Plug-Detect (HPD) device in Qualcomm's downstream
displayport kernel driver. The Type-C Mux is the standard
way that Linux uses to communicate USB-PD altmode events (enter/exit)
to other devices within the kernel. In this driver, Qualcomm
currently only supports using their own PMIC or a single GPIO as
a HPD device. This doesn't work for us since we don't
have Qualcomm's specific PMIC (PM8550B) that handle USB-PD.
And we can't use a GPIO as the HPD device as it doesn't provide enough
information to the DP stack about how it should be configured (number of DP lanes and orientation).
Instead we have a Type-C port manager (CYPD) which communicates
through the Linux Type-C framework. So by adding support for the Type-C Mux
it allows us to connect to use the Type-C framework to receive DP altmode
events from any Type-C connector port manager.

At the moment, this best works with USB to DP cables connected directly to the display.
However, it does also work with USB to HDMI cables (branch devices) but it requires
the display to be on when the cable is plugged into the board. It's also not stable
when connected to a USB Hub with DP/HDMI due to the time it takes to do sink side enumeration.
Currently, if it doesn't detect a sink within 1s, there is no retry mechanism after that
so the user will need to retry the connection. This is just a general bug with DP branch
devices that needs to be worked on in this driver. Basically, if the DP sink is off, the sink count
will always return 0. And if the cable is not actually connected to the display, it will also read 0.
In the latter case we can handle this by using the HPD GPIO as this will be asserted
when the cable is plugged into a display. Handling the former will require some investigation
---
